@@ -69,6 +69,32 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+To pretty print a JSON document supplied as text, use the :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method. It accepts the JSON text, an ``indent`` keyword argument (number of spaces, default 4), and a ``highlight`` keyword argument (default True) which enables syntax highlighting using the console theme::
+
+    console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+Logging a JSON renderable works the same way. The :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable can be passed to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`::
+
+    from rich.json import JSON
+
+    console.log(JSON('{"foo": "bar"}'))
+
+The package also provides a :func:`rich.print_json` function which uses the global console::
+
+    from rich import print_json
+
+    print_json('{"foo": "bar"}')
+
+The JSON renderable disables word wrapping, but default printing still crops lines which are wider than the console. To keep long lines whole, enable soft wrapping, for example ``console.print(JSON(data), soft_wrap=True)``.
+
+JSON can also be pretty printed from the command line by running the ``rich.json`` module with the path to a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json foo.json
+
+
